.claude/plans/PLAN-166/repass-r2/verdict-a.txt
- P1 — Duplicate manifest records are marked invalid, but `_ov_obs_prior_record` ignores `_BASELINE_INVALID` and treats the retained first row as trusted ownership ([scripts/upgrade.sh:990](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/upgrade.sh:990), [scripts/upgrade.sh:1840](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/upgrade.sh:1840)). A duplicate `SPEC/v1/*` row therefore authorizes the forced delete-and-replace path at [scripts/upgrade.sh:2040](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/upgrade.sh:2040), violating fail-closed provenance handling.

- P1 — Downgrading with `--pin` to a pre-marker release drops marker ownership and claims the fallback `VERSION` reflects the pin ([scripts/upgrade.sh:2131](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/upgrade.sh:2131)). The same script explicitly never updates root `VERSION` ([scripts/upgrade.sh:346](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/upgrade.sh:346)), so a downgraded installation reports its stale install-time version, breaking update and forensic version state.

- P2 — An owned but emptied `SPEC/v1` loses ownership on an install rerun: continuity is asserted at [scripts/install.sh:2334](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/install.sh:2334), but the generator emits only files or symlinks and cannot serialize an empty directory ([scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh:178](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/_framework_manifest_set.sh:178)). Subsequent upgrades misclassify the contract as an adopter fork and leave it empty.

The pure ownership oracle and shell syntax pass, but they do not cover these stateful provenance and pin transitions. The duplicate-record path can authorize replacement from explicitly ambiguous input, while the pin path deterministically publishes the wrong installed version; both make rc.2 unsafe to cut.

VERDICT: NEEDS-CHANGES.claude/plans/PLAN-166/repass-r2/verdict-b.txt

- The mtime rider uses whole-second timestamps. A byte-identical refresh occurring in the same second as fixture setup is indistinguishable from preservation, so regressions such as ignoring `--skip` can remain GREEN. The self-check avoids this race by artificially separating timestamps by one minute and therefore does not validate real observation resolution. [test-ownership-table.sh:166](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/tests/test-ownership-table.sh:166), [test-ownership-table.sh:461](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/tests/test-ownership-table.sh:461), [test-ownership-table.sh:741](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/tests/test-ownership-table.sh:741)

P2 findings:

- The user-ceremony transition required by three ownership cells ignores failure, allowing protocol rows to exercise maintainer behavior while reporting GREEN. [test-ownership-table.sh:634](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/tests/test-ownership-table.sh:634)

- The standalone ownership harness exits zero when `AMBIG>0`, contrary to its documented “every row matched” exit contract. The nightly wrapper catches this, but direct release/operator use fails open. [test-ownership-table.sh:696](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/tests/test-ownership-table.sh:696), [test-ownership-table.sh:798](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/tests/test-ownership-table.sh:798)

Justification: Shell syntax, Python parsing, and the committed 65-row/62-green/3-red counts are internally consistent, and the described model-ledger bump raises no independent b/5 concern. However, the parity and ownership gates can certify concrete deletion, structural-delivery, and coverage regressions, so this payload cannot safely gate rc.2.

VERDICT: NEEDS-CHANGES.claude/plans/PLAN-166/repass-r2/verdict-c.txt
- **P1 — A negative verdict can authorize the release.** The new guard parses the YAML but never checks `fields["verdict"]`; it begins enforcement with `release_tag` at [.claude/scripts/local/_release_tag_guard.py:285](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/.claude/scripts/local/_release_tag_guard.py:285). Thus `verdict: NO-GO`, an unknown value, or a missing verdict receives the same decision as `GO` when the remaining fields are valid. The driver then signs the tag at [.claude/scripts/local/release.sh:630](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/.claude/scripts/local/release.sh:630). The downstream pair-rail validator also does not inspect the verdict value, so `release-gate` can succeed and the new poller can grant npm publication despite a cross-model NO-GO. Require an explicitly release-authorizing verdict—normally exact `GO`—before accepting the delta or signing.

- **P2 — Malformed candidate-job input is classified as WAIT.** [.claude/scripts/await_release_gate.py:294](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/.claude/scripts/await_release_gate.py:294) maps any non-list `jobs` value to “not materialised,” and [.claude/scripts/await_release_gate.py:338](/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/.claude/scripts/await_release_gate.py:338) consequently returns WAIT. A direct `jobs: "corrupt"` reproduction confirmed this. It eventually blocks at the deadline and never grants, so this is fix-forward, but it contradicts the script’s malformed-input BLOCK contract.

The P1 breaks the fundamental all-payloads-must-approve invariant and can propagate through the complete release-to-publish chain. Syntax, stdlib-only, contamination, AGENTS freshness, count-claim, and environment-hygiene checks otherwise passed; the described model-deprecation replacements raised no additional issue.

VERDICT: NEEDS-CHANGES.claude/plans/PLAN-166/repass-r2/verdict-d.txt
- P1 — `.claude/scripts/tests/test_inject_agent_context_exact_resolution.py:29` forwards the ambient environment, including real `HOME`, `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`, and `CEO_*`, while line 42 uses bare `unittest.TestCase`. Direct execution via line 92 bypasses pytest’s suite isolation; the invoked script calls `lessons.py --emit-consumer`, potentially reading or writing the operator’s real lesson/audit state. This violates the mandatory environment-isolation rule.

- P2 — `.claude/scripts/tests/test_release_workflow_asserts.py:129` treats absence of the live PLAN-166 marker as “pre-landing”; line 503 then skips the await-gate tests. The W1-B checks repeat this at lines 718–754. Removing a live gate together with its marker therefore produces skips rather than failures; a mutation probe confirmed one skip with zero failures.

- P2 — `.claude/scripts/tests/test_inject_agent_context_exact_resolution.py:81` accepts every exit code except 2 for slash/ampersand names. An unrelated crash with exit 1 passed this test during review. Acceptable outcomes should be constrained to successful resolution or the documented resolution failure.

- P3 — `.claude/scripts/tests/test_release_workflow_asserts.py:1005` claims to check every failure code for uniqueness but omits `E_PARENT_NOT_ANCESTOR`.

Justification: The live workflow assertions and pure await-gate cases passed read-only structural execution, Python 3.9 parsing and stdlib checks were clean, and the described model-ledger replacement bump presents no additional concern. The full scoped pytest run could not create temporary fixtures in the read-only reviewer sandbox. The new real-environment test violates an explicit release-review rule and must be corrected before the cut.

VERDICT: NEEDS-CHANGES.claude/plans/PLAN-166/repass-r2/verdict-e.txt

- **P1 — npm provenance instructions provide false assurance.** [docs/CTO-GUIDE.md:159](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/docs/CTO-GUIDE.md:159>) executes the package through npm’s temporary exec cache, then [line 160](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/docs/CTO-GUIDE.md:160>) claims `npm audit signatures` verifies it. That audit loads the current project’s installed dependency tree, not the ephemeral `npm exec` package. The repository’s correct procedure installs the exact package into an isolated tree with scripts disabled before auditing it at [.github/workflows/supply-chain-watch.yml:147](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/.github/workflows/supply-chain-watch.yml:147>). As written, users execute the artifact before performing any meaningful provenance verification.

- **P2 — the documented per-command overhead override cannot work.** [docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md:74](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.md:74>) and [docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.pt-BR.md:75](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/docs/TROUBLESHOOTING.pt-BR.md:75>) recommend an inline shell assignment, but the PreToolUse hook reads only its own ambient environment before Bash executes at [.claude/hooks/check_anti_ceo_overhead.py:733](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/.claude/hooks/check_anti_ceo_overhead.py:733>).

- **P2 — current-fleet cost disclosure contradicts itself.** [docs/cost-of-operation.md:49](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/docs/cost-of-operation.md:49>) says mitigated agents inherit a Fable 5 CEO, then claims they bill at $5/$25, while [line 105](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/docs/cost-of-operation.md:105>) prices Fable 5 at $10/$50. That can understate inherited cost by 2×.

- **P3 — stale nightly census comments.** [.github/workflows/ownership-nightly.yml:3](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/.github/workflows/ownership-nightly.yml:3>) says 62 cells and four expected reds; the TSV contains 65 cells and [scripts/tests/ownership-expected-reds.txt:13](</private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/repass-r2.nPXKYg/wt/scripts/tests/ownership-expected-reds.txt:13>) lists three. The executable gate reads the files dynamically, so enforcement remains intact.

The excluded model-deprecations replacement-only update raises no finding as described. Workflow linting, diff hygiene, count checkers, and the major on-disk count claims passed; the new release gates are structurally fail-closed. Nevertheless, the P1 publishes a supply-chain verification procedure that does not verify the artifact it tells adopters to execute, so rc.2 should not be cut until that guidance is corrected.

